10	    },
11	    {
12	      "claim_id": "ATT-0140-CLM-02",
13	      "note": "Partial normalization along a distinguished non-separating node yields a connected stable genus-5 curve with the original six ordered marks plus the two unordered preimages of the node, so n=6+2=8 and dim Mbar_{5,8}=20=21-1. Ordering the two preimages is an S_2-torsor on the ordered atlas Mbar_{5,8}; clutching the last two sections is inverse in families. The induced map [Mbar_{5,8}/S_2] -> Delta_irr is finite birational from a normal stack, hence the normalization. This is stack-theoretic, not a coarse or ordered identification; the ordered clutching map is generically degree two and is not itself the normalization. Independently matches SRC-0003: the one-edge loop term is H^*(Mbar_{g-1,n+2})^{S_2} with Aut=S_2 swapping the two half-edges.",
14	      "verdict": "confirmed"
15	    },
16	    {
17	      "claim_id": "ATT-0140-CLM-03",
18	      "note": "A distinguished separating node splits into two stable pointed components with g1+g2=6 and n1+n2=6+2=8. Identifications with a fixed one-edge stable graph Gamma form an Aut(Gamma)-torsor, and clutching is inverse, including with monodromy of graph identifications, so the component is the quotient stack [Mbar_Gamma/Aut(Gamma)]. Because the six legs are labeled, Aut(Gamma) cannot exchange vertices (an automorphism must fix each labeled leg), so Aut(Gamma) is in fact trivial for every separating one-edge graph in this moduli problem; retaining the quotient notation is the canonical graph construction and is isomorphic to the ordered product atlas. Stability of both vertices is the vertex-stability condition in Gamma.",
