 so Phi_{g,n}^k=0. Therefore W_{vcd} H^{vcd}(M_{g,n};Q)=Psi_{g,n}^{vcd}=sum_i psi_i sum_j pi_j^* W_{vcd-2} H^{vcd-2}(M_{g,n-1};Q). The comparison vcd(M_{g,n})-n=4g-4=vcd(M_g)+1 is independent of n>=1. Each psi_i is algebraic of type Q(-1). Whenever n-1<=c(g) in the published CKgP table c(3)=c(4)=11, c(5)=7, c(6)=5, c(7)=3, Canning-Larson-Payne Proposition 4.5 implies that W_{vcd-2} H^{vcd-2}(M_{g,n-1};Q) is algebraic of Tate type, and semisimplicity of polarizable pure Q-Hodge structures of a single weight realises W_{vcd} H^{vcd}(M_{g,n};Q) as a finite direct sum of Q(-vcd/2). In particular, at the campaign pair (g,n)=(6,6) one has k=26, n-1=5=c(6), and W_26 H^{26}(M_{6,6};Q) is a finite direct sum of Q(-13). The same peel does not kill the primitive term in degree vcd-1 and does not apply where no published c(g) is available.
