  "summary": "For every one-edge separating stable graph Gamma of (6,6) the automorphism group is trivial (labelled legs cannot cross a separating edge), so the determinant-edge twist is vacuous and the contribution is the untwisted H^{16} of the ordered atlas Mbar_{g1,n1} x Mbar_{g2,n2}. In every Kunneth summand H^a tensor H^b with a+b=16 and a,b >= 1, either both degrees are odd, in which case the smaller is an odd degree <= 7 and the factor vanishes (SRC-0003 L15-16, Bergstrom-Faber-Payne odd vanishing for odd k <= 9), or both are even and <= 14, in which case both factors are Tate by THM-0002 (SRC-0002 Theorem 1.5(3)). The remaining summands are H^{16} tensor H^0. Every vertex satisfies g_i <= 6, n_i <= 7 and 2g_i+n_i <= 17, because 2g_1+n_1+2g_2+n_2 = 20 and each stable vertex has 2g+n >= 3. Both g and 2g+n are non-increasing along boundary degeneration (2g+n drops strictly at each separating step), so the entire degree-16 boundary induction tree of every vertex stays in the range g <= 6, 2g+n <= 17; in that range the CKgP bound n <= c(g) holds exactly (c(3)=c(4)=11, c(5)=7, c(6)=5), genus 1 and 2 are discharged by THM-0006 and THM-0007, and no pair of the RED-0001 residual list occurs, since each residual pair has g >= 7 or 2g+n = 18. An induction on dim Mbar_{g,n} using the THM-0003 boundary sequence, CLP Proposition 4.5 and FND-0001 duality then gives H^{16}(Mbar_{g,n};Q) = Q(-8)^{oplus} in that range, which supplies the H^{16} tensor H^0 summands. Hence every Aut(Gamma)-invariant determinant-edge Kunneth summand in degree 16 is a finite direct sum of Q(-8). The non-separating component and the boundary-image theorem are untouched.",
  "argument_markdown": "## 0. Conventions, and the exact statement in the form proved\n\nWork over `C` with rational coefficients on complex Deligne-Mumford (DM) stacks. `Q(m)` is the Tate Hodge structure, pure of weight `-2m` and Hodge type `(-m,-m)`. For a pure weight-16 structure `V`, the assertions 'V is a finite direct sum of Q(-8)' and 'V_C = V^{8,8}' are the same condition.\n\nFor a smooth proper DM stack `X` over `C`, `H^k(X;Q)` is a pure polarizable Hodge structure of weight `k` (registry claim THM-0001, source_verified). Put `H_k(X;Q) := Hom(H^k(X;Q),Q)`, pure polarizable of weight `-k`. Therefore\n\n> (0.1) `H^k(X;Q)` is a finite direct sum of `Q(-k/2)` if and only if `H_k(X;Q)` is a finite direct sum of `Q(k/2)`.\n\nAll Kunneth bookkeeping below is done in ORDINARY cohomology in total degree 16 on the ordered product atlas. Homology is used only through (0.1), and only inside Step 7 where the boundary sequence is applied. The covariant degree-24 form of the same data is never mixed into a degree-16 computation, and the ambient Poincare twist (21) of `Mbar_{6,6}` is never applied to a 20-dimensional boundary component.\n\n**(P) Semisimplicity.** Polarizable pure `Q`-Hodge structures of a fixed weight form a semisimple abelian category. We use only: (P1) every sub-object and every quotient object of `Q(-8)^{oplus a}` is `Q(-8)^{oplus b}` with `b <= a`, and dually for `Q(8)`; (P2) an extension of `Q(8)^{oplus a}` by `Q(8)^{oplus b}` inside weight `-16` splits. FND-0033 records exactly this for the present corner: at a single pure weight there is no non-split-extension obstruction, and no gap marker is warranted for it.\n\n**Exact theorem (assigned, proved below).** *Using a verified C66BAR-GRAPH identification of the one-edge separating components [Mbar_Gamma/Aut(Gamma)], prove that every Aut(Gamma)-invariant determinant-edge Kunneth summand in degree 16 is a finite direct sum of Q(-8). On the ordered product atlases, the only degree-16 factors that arise have n<=7 and lie outside RED-0001; even degrees <=14 are in the published tautological range. This theorem does not treat the non-separating component [Mbar_{5,8}/S_2] and does not conclude the campaign boundary-image theorem.*\n\nFormal reading proved here. Let `Gamma` be a one-edge SEPARATING stable graph for `(g,n)=(6,6)` with vertices `(g_1,n_1)`, `(g_2,n_2)`, `g_1+g_2=6`, `n_1+n_2=8`, both stable, and let `Mbar_Gamma = Mbar_{g_1,n_1} x Mbar_{g_2,n_2}` be the ordered product atlas. Then:\n\n- (T1) `Aut(Gamma)=1`, `det E_Gamma` is a one-dimensional trivial-Hodge-structure representation of the trivial group, and `(H^{16}(Mbar_Gamma;Q) tensor det E_Gamma)^{Aut(Gamma)} = H^{16}(Mbar_Gamma;Q) = H^{16}([Mbar_Gamma/Aut(Gamma)];Q)`;\n- (T2) for every pair of integers `a,b >= 0` with `a+b=16`, the Kunneth summand `H^a(Mbar_{g_1,n_1};Q) tensor H^b(Mbar_{g_2,n_2};Q)` is a finite direct sum of `Q(-8)` (possibly zero); hence so is `H^{16}([Mbar_Gamma/Aut(Gamma)];Q)`;\n- (T3) the only degree-16 FACTORS occurring, i.e. the groups `H^{16}(Mbar_{g_i,n_i};Q)` paired with `H^0` of the other vertex, have `n_i <= 7`, and `(g_i,n_i)` lies outside the RED-0001 residual list; indeed every stable pair occurring anywhere in the degree-16 boundary induction of `(g_i,n_i)` lies outside that list;\n- (T4) every Kunneth summand with `a,b >= 1` has both degrees even and `<= 14`, or vanishes; the even ones are exactly the published tautological range of THM-0002.\n\nExclusions are as stated in the theorem and are listed in `theorem_scope.excluded`; they are honoured throughout and re-examined in Step 10.\n\n## 1. Inputs, with provenance, separated by epistemic status\n\n**(D) Dependency artifacts (subproblem C66BAR-GRAPH; supplied by TASK.json and read in full together with both confirmations).** ATT-0140 (`proof/attempts/ATT-0140.json`), double-confirmed by REV-0189 (pass 1, grok) and REV-0191 (pass 2, claude), both verdict `confirmed` on every claim and on the dependency check. Used:\n\n- ATT-0140-CLM-01: the normalization of the reduced boundary of `Mbar_{6,6}` is the stack of stable 6-pointed genus-6 curves with a distinguished node;\n- ATT-0140-CLM-03: for every one-edge separating stable graph `Gamma` the corresponding normalized component is `[Mbar_Gamma/Aut(Gamma)]` with `Mbar_Gamma = Mbar_{g_1,n_1} x Mbar_{g_2,n_2}`, `g_1+g_2=6`, `n_1+n_2=8`, both vertices stable;\n- ATT-0140-CLM-04: the contribution of a one-edge graph is `(H^*(Mbar_Gamma;Q) tensor det E_Gamma)^{Aut(Gamma)}`, and for finite `G` acting on a DM stack `Y` one has `H^*([Y/G];Q) = H^*(Y;Q)^G` by Cartan-Leray plus averaging over `Q`.\n\n**(R) Project claim-registry inputs (`repo/data/claims.jsonl`), all with truth_status `established`.**\n\n- THM-0001 (source_verified): `H^k` of a smooth proper DM stack over `C` is pure of weight `k`.\n- THM-0002 (cross_checked): for all stable `(g,n)`, `H^k(Mbar_{g,n};Q)` is a direct sum of `Q(-k/2)` for even `k <= 14`. Source text: SRC-0002 Theorem 1.5(3), `repo/corpus/text/SRC-0002.txt` L81-L84 ('The tautological ring RH^*(Mbar_{g,n}) contains ... (3) H^k(Mbar_{g,n}), for even k <= 14, for all g and n') and L92 ('Theorem 1.5(3) implies that H^k(Mbar_{g,n}) = sum L^{k/2} for even k <= 14').\n- THM-0003 (source_verified): the boundary long exact sequence gives, for all `(g,n)` and all `k`, right exactness `H_k(hat-partial Mbar_{g,n};Q) -> H_k(Mbar_{g,n};Q) -> W_{-k}H^{BM}_k(M_{g,n};Q) -> 0` (SRC-0002 Eq. (6.1); the `k=16`, `(6,6)` instance is the packet's boundary sequence).\n- THM-0005 (cross_checked): `M_{g,n}` has the CKgP for `n <= c(g)`, with `c(3)=c(4)=11`, `c(5)=7`, `c(6)=5`, `c(7)=3`, genus 0 unrestricted. Source text: SRC-0002 Table 1, `repo/corpus/text/SRC-0002.txt` L638-L641 ('g 0 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 / c(g) inf 10 10 11 11 7 5 3 / Table 1. M_{g,n} has the CKgP and A^*(M_{g,n}) = R^*(M_{g,n}), for n <= c(g)'). Only the entries `c(0)=inf`, `c(3)=c(4)=11`, `c(5)=7`, `c(6)=5` are used.\n- THM-0006 (source_verified): for every `n`, all even cohomology of `Mbar_{1,n}` is represented by tautological boundary classes. Source text: SRC-0002 `repo/corpus/text/SRC-0002.txt` L332 ('By [26], all even cohomology in genus 1 is represented by boundary strata').\n- THM-0007 (source_verified): for `k <= 10` and all `n`, `H^{2k}(Mbar_{2,n};Q) = RH^{2k}(Mbar_{2,n})`. Source text: SRC-0002 Corollary 2.7, `repo/corpus/text/SRC-0002.txt` L362.\n\n**(S) Source-backed inputs quoted verbatim from the project corpus (published theorems, cited not re-proved).**\n\n- (S1) SRC-0002 Proposition 4.5, `repo/corpus/text/SRC-0002.txt` L643-L648: 'For all g <= 7 and n <= c(g) as specified in Table 1, we have W_kH^k(M_{g,n}) = RH^k(M_{g,n}).' Used only for `g` in `{0,3,4,5,6}`.\n- (S2) SRC-0003, `repo/corpus/text/SRC-0003.txt` L15-L16: 'Bergstrom, Faber and the third author recently proved that H^k(Mbar_{g,n}) vanishes for all odd k <= 9 and all g and n [4].' Used only in the form: `H^k(Mbar_{g,n};Q)=0` for odd `k` in `{1,3,5,7}`, for all stable `(g,n)`. (SRC-0003 Theorem 1.2, L48, 'For any even k <= 12, the cohomology group H^k(Mbar_{g,n}) is pure Hodge-Tate', is not needed, being weaker than THM-0002 in the range used.)\n- Not used, but recorded to prevent over-reach: SRC-0002 L93 states that degrees `k = 16,18,20` are PREDICTED (Chenevier-Lannes), not proved; OBS-0001 records the same. Nothing below assumes a universal degree-16 statement.\n\n**(F) Adjudicated packet findings used.** FND-0001 (mechanically_verified, cases: all): for `d = 3g-3+n`, `W_{-16}H^{BM}_{16}(M_{g,n};Q)` is identified by Poincare duality with `W_{2d-16}H^{2d-16}(M_{g,n};Q)(d)`. FND-0033 (corroborated): semisimplicity at a fixed pure weight, no extension gap.\n\n**Not used as a proof input: RED-0001.** RED-0001 has truth_status `inferred`. Nothing below is derived from it. It is used only in Step 9 as the ENUMERATION of the seven residual pairs `(3,12),(5,8),(6,6),(7,4),(8,0),(8,1),(8,2)`, in order to verify the theorem's own bookkeeping clause 'lie outside RED-0001'; the disjointness is then established by the numerical criterion of Step 5, not by citing RED-0001's status. Likewise the C66-001 open residual (ATT-0136) is neither used nor re-proved.\n\n## 2. The one-edge separating graphs of (6,6), and Aut(Gamma) = 1\n\nBy ATT-0140-CLM-01 and ATT-0140-CLM-03 (confirmed by REV-0189 and REV-0191) the separating part of `hat-partial Mbar_{6,6}` is the disjoint union, over one-edge separating stable graphs `Gamma`, of the quotient stacks `[Mbar_Gamma/Aut(Gamma)]`. Concretely, `Gamma` is a tree with two vertices `v_1,v_2` joined by one edge, so `h^1(Gamma)=0` and `g_1+g_2=6`; the six labelled legs are partitioned as `S_1 sqcup S_2`, and each vertex carries in addition the half-edge of the node, so `n_i = |S_i|+1` and `n_1+n_2 = 6+2 = 8`. Vertex stability is `2g_i-2+n_i > 0`.\n\n**Claim: `Aut(Gamma) = 1`.** An automorphism of a stable graph fixes every labelled leg. It either fixes both vertices or exchanges them. If it exchanged `v_1` and `v_2`, it would carry the leg set `S_1` bijectively onto `S_2` while fixing each leg, forcing `S_1 = S_2`; since `S_1` and `S_2` are disjoint this forces `S_1 = S_2 = empty`, i.e. `n=0`, contradicting `n=6`. Hence both vertices are fixed. The two half-edges of a separating edge lie on distinct vertices, so they are then fixed as well, and the automorphism is the identity. (This reproves, independently, the subsidiary remark of ATT-0140 checked and confirmed in REV-0189 note CLM-03 and REV-0191 note CLM-03.) Note the argument uses `n >= 1` and labelled legs; it genuinely fails for `n = 0` with `g_1 = g_2`, and is not asserted there.\n\nConsequently `[Mbar_Gamma/Aut(Gamma)] = Mbar_Gamma = Mbar_{g_1,n_1} x Mbar_{g_2,n_2}` canonically, and by ATT-0140-CLM-04 (Cartan-Leray with `Q` coefficients) `H^*([Mbar_Gamma/Aut(Gamma)];Q) = H^*(Mbar_Gamma;Q)^{Aut(Gamma)} = H^*(Mbar_Gamma;Q)`.\n\n## 3. The determinant-edge convention is vacuous on the separating side\n\n`det E_Gamma` is a one-dimensional `Q`-vector space with trivial Hodge structure, carrying an action of `Aut(Gamma)` (by ATT-0140-CLM-04 and both confirmations it is the determinant of the permutation representation on the singleton edge set, hence trivial). Since `Aut(Gamma) = 1` by Step 2,\n\n> `(H^{16}(Mbar_Gamma;Q) tensor det E_Gamma)^{Aut(Gamma)} = H^{16}(Mbar_Gamma;Q) tensor det E_Gamma ~= H^{16}(Mbar_Gamma;Q)`\n\nas Hodge structures, and this holds under ANY convention for the `Aut(Gamma)`-action on `det E_Gamma`, because the acting group is trivial. In particular the separating-side conclusion of this artifact is logically independent of the disputed parenthetical of FND-0034 (that the determinant twist 'kills contributions whose automorphisms ... swap the two branches of the non-separating node') and of the correcting candidate FC-REV0191-1 in REV-0191. That dispute concerns the non-separating loop, which this theorem explicitly does not treat. This proves (T1).\n\nFor later use in Step 7 we record the convention-free bound that is all any induction needs: for a finite group `G` acting on a `Q`-Hodge structure `V` and any one-dimensional `Q[G]`-module `chi` with trivial Hodge structure, `(V tensor chi)^G` is a sub-Hodge-structure of `V tensor chi ~= V`. Hence if `V` is a finite direct sum of `Q(-8)`, so is `(V tensor chi)^G`, by (P1).\n\n## 4. Numerical constraints on the vertices\n\nSet `m(g,n) := 2g+n` and `d(g,n) := 3g-3+n = dim Mbar_{g,n}`. For a one-edge separating graph of `(6,6)`:\n\n- `m_1 + m_2 = 2(g_1+g_2) + (n_1+n_2) = 12 + 8 = 20` and `d_1 + d_2 = 3*6-6+8-3+3 = 20`, consistent with `dim [Mbar_Gamma/Aut(Gamma)] = 20 = 21-1`.\n- Stability `2g_i-2+n_i > 0` gives the integer bound `m_i = 2g_i+n_i >= 3`, with equality exactly for `(0,3)` and `(1,1)`. Hence `m_i <= 20-3 = 17` for `i=1,2`.\n- `n_i <= 7`, because `n_1+n_2 = 8` and `n_j >= 1` (each vertex carries the node half-edge as a marking).\n- `g_i <= 6`, because `g_1+g_2 = 6` and `g_j >= 0`.\n\nBoth extreme values are attained: `(6,5)` paired with `(0,3)` and `(5,7)` paired with `(1,1)` both have `m_i = 17`. Note that `(6,6)` cannot occur as a vertex, since it would force the other vertex to be `(0,2)`, which is unstable; and `(5,8)` cannot occur, since `n_i <= 7`.\n\n## 5. Monotonicity of `(g, 2g+n)` under boundary degeneration\n\n**Lemma M.** Let `(g,n)` be stable and let `(g',n')` be the pair attached to any vertex of any stable graph of genus `g` with `n` labelled legs. Then `g' <= g` and `m(g',n') <= m(g,n)`. Moreover, for a one-edge SEPARATING graph each vertex satisfies `m_i <= m(g,n) - 1`, and for the one-edge non-separating graph the unique vertex `(g-1,n+2)` satisfies `g' = g-1` and `m(g',n') = m(g,n)`. In all one-edge cases `d(g',n') <= d(g,n)-1`.\n\n*Proof.* One-edge cases first. Non-separating: `(g',n') = (g-1,n+2)`, so `g' = g-1 <= g`, `m' = 2(g-1)+(n+2) = 2g+n = m`, and `d' = 3(g-1)-3+(n+2) = d-1`. Separating: `g_1+g_2 = g` and `n_1+n_2 = n+2`, so `g_i <= g` and `m_1+m_2 = 2g+n+2 = m+2`; since the complementary vertex is stable, `m_j >= 3`, whence `m_i <= m-1`. Also `d_1+d_2 = 3g-6+n+2 = d-1`, and `d_j >= 0`, so `d_i <= d-1`.\n\nGeneral stable graphs follow by induction on the number of edges. If `Gamma` has `E >= 1` edges, choose an edge `e` and contract all other edges; the result is a one-edge stable graph `Gamma_e` of `(g,n)`, and `Gamma` is obtained by replacing each vertex `w` of `Gamma_e` by a stable graph of the pair attached to `w`, whose edges are the edges of `Gamma` other than `e`. Every vertex of `Gamma` is therefore a vertex of a stable graph of some vertex pair of `Gamma_e`, and the inequalities compose because both `g` and `m` are non-increasing at each one-edge step. QED\n\n**Corollary M1.** Every stable pair occurring at a vertex of any stable graph of a `(6,6)`-vertex `(g_i,n_i)` satisfies `g' <= g_i <= 6` and `m(g',n') <= m_i <= 17`.\n\n**Corollary M2 (CKgP coverage is exactly tight in this range).** If `(g',n')` is stable with `g' <= 6` and `m(g',n') <= 17`, then `n' <= c(g')` whenever `g'` is in `{0,3,4,5,6}`. Indeed `c(0) = inf`; for `g' = 3`, `n' <= 17-6 = 11 = c(3)`; for `g' = 4`, `n' <= 17-8 = 9 <= 11 = c(4)`; for `g' = 5`, `n' <= 17-10 = 7 = c(5)`; for `g' = 6`, `n' <= 17-12 = 5 = c(6)`. The bounds for `g' = 3,5,6` are attained, so the range `m <= 17` is exactly the CKgP-covered range for genus at most 6, not a slack estimate.\n\n## 6. Proposition K: the degree-16 Kunneth summands of a product atlas\n\n**Proposition K.** Let `(A_1,A_2) = ((g_1,n_1),(g_2,n_2))` be stable pairs such that `H^{16}(Mbar_{g_i,n_i};Q)` is a finite direct sum of `Q(-8)` for `i = 1,2`. Then for every `a,b >= 0` with `a+b = 16` the summand `H^a(Mbar_{g_1,n_1};Q) tensor H^b(Mbar_{g_2,n_2};Q)` is a finite direct sum of `Q(-8)` (possibly zero), and hence so is `H^{16}(Mbar_{g_1,n_1} x Mbar_{g_2,n_2};Q)`.\n\n*Proof.* The Kunneth isomorphism `H^{16}(X x Y;Q) = oplus_{a+b=16} H^a(X;Q) tensor H^b(Y;Q)` is an isomorphism of Hodge structures for smooth proper DM stacks over `C`, and all groups are finite-dimensional. Enumerate the summands.\n\n1. `a` odd (equivalently `b` odd, since `a+b=16` is even). Then `min(a,b) <= 8`, and being odd, `min(a,b)` lies in `{1,3,5,7}`. By input (S2) the corresponding factor vanishes, so the summand is `0`. The eight ordered odd cases are `(1,15),(3,13),(5,11),(7,9),(9,7),(11,5),(13,3),(15,1)`, and in each the smaller degree is `1,3,5` or `7`. This is the only place odd degrees enter, and the input is used only for odd `k <= 7`; no vanishing is claimed or needed in odd degrees `9,11,13,15`. (This matters: SRC-0002 L97-L99 records that `H^{13}` and `H^{15}` of `Mbar_{g,n}` lie in subtautological extensions generated by genus-1 cohomology, and SRC-0003 Theorem 1.1 records `H^{11}(Mbar_{1,n}) != 0` for `n >= 11`, so no universal odd vanishing above degree 9 is available or assumed.)\n2. `a,b` both even with `a,b >= 2`. Then `2 <= a,b <= 14`, so THM-0002 gives `H^a(Mbar_{g_1,n_1};Q) = Q(-a/2)^{oplus p}` and `H^b(Mbar_{g_2,n_2};Q) = Q(-b/2)^{oplus q}`, whence the summand is `Q(-a/2-b/2)^{oplus pq} = Q(-8)^{oplus pq}`. This is clause (T4): every mixed summand lives in the published tautological range of even degrees at most 14.\n3. `(a,b) = (16,0)` or `(0,16)`. `Mbar_{g,n}` is connected, so `H^0(Mbar_{g,n};Q) = Q = Q(0)`, and the summand is `H^{16}(Mbar_{g_i,n_i};Q) tensor Q(0)`, a finite direct sum of `Q(-8)` by hypothesis.\n\nA finite direct sum of finite direct sums of `Q(-8)` is a finite direct sum of `Q(-8)`. QED\n\n## 7. Lemma A: degree-16 Tate type in the range `g <= 6`, `2g+n <= 17`\n\n**Lemma A.** Let `(g,n)` be stable with `g <= 6` and `m(g,n) = 2g+n <= 17`. Then `H^{16}(Mbar_{g,n};Q)` is a finite direct sum of `Q(-8)`.\n\n*Proof.* Induction on `d = d(g,n) = dim Mbar_{g,n} >= 0`.\n\nIf `2d < 16` then `H^{16}(Mbar_{g,n};Q) = 0` and there is nothing to prove; this covers all `d <= 7`, in particular the base cases `Mbar_{0,3}` and `Mbar_{1,1}`. So assume `d >= 8`.\n\nCase `g = 1`. By THM-0006 all even cohomology of `Mbar_{1,n}` is represented by tautological boundary classes. A class represented by an algebraic cycle of codimension 8 has Hodge type `(8,8)`; if `H^{16}` is spanned by such classes then, being pure of weight 16 (THM-0001) with `H^{16}_C = (H^{16})^{8,8}`, it is a finite direct sum of `Q(-8)`. No constraint on `n` is used.\n\nCase `g = 2`. By THM-0007 with `k = 8 <= 10`, `H^{16}(Mbar_{2,n};Q) = RH^{16}(Mbar_{2,n})` for all `n`, and the same algebraicity argument applies.\n\nCase `g` in `{0,3,4,5,6}`. By Corollary M2, `n <= c(g)`. We use the boundary sequence and the induction hypothesis.\n\n(7a) *The normalized boundary and its degree-16 homology.* For every stable `(g,n)`, the normalization `hat-partial Mbar_{g,n}` of the reduced boundary is the disjoint union, over one-edge stable graphs `Gamma` of `(g,n)`, of `[Mbar_Gamma/Aut(Gamma)]`, and `H^*([Mbar_Gamma/Aut(Gamma)];Q) = H^*(Mbar_Gamma;Q)^{Aut(Gamma)}`, the determinant-edge form of the contribution being `(H^*(Mbar_Gamma;Q) tensor det E_Gamma)^{Aut(Gamma)}`. This is exactly ATT-0140-CLM-01, -03, -04, whose proof is uniform in `(g,n)`: etale-locally the boundary is the reduced normal-crossings divisor `prod_e t_e = 0` in independent node-smoothing parameters, so its normalization is the stack of stable curves with a distinguished node; partial normalization at the distinguished node and clutching are mutually inverse in families and exhibit the component of combinatorial type `Gamma` as the quotient of the ordered atlas `Mbar_Gamma` by the `Aut(Gamma)`-torsor of graph identifications; and rational cohomology of a finite quotient stack is invariant cohomology by Cartan-Leray plus averaging. ATT-0140 states this for `(6,6)` and is double-confirmed (REV-0189, REV-0191); no step of that argument uses `g = 6` or `n = 6`. Only an upper bound is needed here, and by the convention-free remark at the end of Step 3, `(H_{16}(Mbar_Gamma;Q) tensor det E_Gamma)^{Aut(Gamma)}` is a sub-Hodge-structure of `H_{16}(Mbar_Gamma;Q)` whatever `Aut(Gamma)` and whatever the determinant convention. In particular nothing here depends on the disputed non-separating branch-swap sign, and no `Aut(Gamma)` is computed.\n\n(7b) *Each one-edge atlas is Tate in degree 16.* Let `Gamma` be a one-edge stable graph of `(g,n)`. If `Gamma` is non-separating, `Mbar_Gamma = Mbar_{g-1,n+2}` with `g-1 <= 6`, `m(g-1,n+2) = m(g,n) <= 17` and `d(g-1,n+2) = d-1` (Lemma M); the induction hypothesis gives `H^{16}(Mbar_Gamma;Q) = Q(-8)^{oplus}`. If `Gamma` is separating with vertices `(g_1,n_1),(g_2,n_2)`, then by Lemma M each vertex satisfies `g_i <= g <= 6`, `m_i <= m-1 <= 16` and `d_i <= d-1`, so the induction hypothesis applies to both vertices and Proposition K gives `H^{16}(Mbar_Gamma;Q) = Q(-8)^{oplus}`. In both cases, by (0.1), `H_{16}(Mbar_Gamma;Q) = Q(8)^{oplus}`, and by (P1) and (7a) the contribution of `Gamma` to `H_{16}(hat-partial Mbar_{g,n};Q)` is `Q(8)^{oplus}`. There are finitely many one-edge stable graphs, so `H_{16}(hat-partial Mbar_{g,n};Q) = Q(8)^{oplus}`.\n\n(7c) *The open input.* Let `d = 3g-3+n`. Since `g <= 7` and `n <= c(g)`, input (S1) (SRC-0002 Proposition 4.5) gives `W_{2d-16}H^{2d-16}(M_{g,n};Q) = RH^{2d-16}(M_{g,n})`, which is spanned by algebraic classes of codimension `d-8`, hence is a finite direct sum of `Q(-(d-8))`. By FND-0001, `W_{-16}H^{BM}_{16}(M_{g,n};Q) ~= W_{2d-16}H^{2d-16}(M_{g,n};Q)(d)`, and `Q(-(d-8))(d) = Q(8)`. Hence `W_{-16}H^{BM}_{16}(M_{g,n};Q)` is a finite direct sum of `Q(8)`. (If `2d-16 < 0` the group vanishes and the conclusion is trivial.)\n\n(7d) *Assembly.* THM-0003 gives the right exact sequence `H_{16}(hat-partial Mbar_{g,n};Q) -> H_{16}(Mbar_{g,n};Q) -> W_{-16}H^{BM}_{16}(M_{g,n};Q) -> 0` of Hodge structures. All three terms are pure of weight `-16`: the first two because `hat-partial Mbar_{g,n}` and `Mbar_{g,n}` are smooth and proper (THM-0001 and (0.1)), the third because it is a graded piece of a mixed Hodge structure. The image `I` of the first map is a quotient of `Q(8)^{oplus}` by (7b), hence `I = Q(8)^{oplus}` by (P1); the cokernel is `Q(8)^{oplus}` by (7c); so `H_{16}(Mbar_{g,n};Q)` is an extension of `Q(8)^{oplus}` by `Q(8)^{oplus}` at the single weight `-16`, hence `Q(8)^{oplus}` by (P2). By (0.1), `H^{16}(Mbar_{g,n};Q)` is a finite direct sum of `Q(-8)`. QED\n\n*Non-circularity.* The induction strictly decreases `d`, and by Lemma M it never leaves the range `g <= 6`, `m <= 17`. By Step 9 that range excludes all seven RED-0001 residual pairs; in particular the campaign target `(6,6)` and the pair `(5,8)` never appear, so Lemma A neither presupposes nor re-proves any residual entry, and it does not use RED-0001, THM-0004, OBS-0001, or the degree-16 prediction of SRC-0002 L93.\n\n## 8. Proof of the theorem\n\nLet `Gamma` be a one-edge separating stable graph of `(6,6)` with vertices `(g_1,n_1)`, `(g_2,n_2)`.\n\nBy Step 4, `g_i <= 6` and `m_i = 2g_i+n_i <= 17` for `i = 1,2`. Hence Lemma A applies to both vertices and `H^{16}(Mbar_{g_i,n_i};Q)` is a finite direct sum of `Q(-8)`.\n\nBy Proposition K, every Kunneth summand `H^a(Mbar_{g_1,n_1};Q) tensor H^b(Mbar_{g_2,n_2};Q)` with `a+b=16` is a finite direct sum of `Q(-8)`, and so is their sum `H^{16}(Mbar_Gamma;Q)`. This is (T2).\n\nBy Steps 2 and 3, `Aut(Gamma) = 1` and the determinant-edge twist is vacuous, so the `Aut(Gamma)`-invariant determinant-edge summands ARE the Kunneth summands just typed, and `(H^{16}(Mbar_Gamma;Q) tensor det E_Gamma)^{Aut(Gamma)} = H^{16}(Mbar_Gamma;Q) = H^{16}([Mbar_Gamma/Aut(Gamma)];Q)` is a finite direct sum of `Q(-8)`. This is (T1) together with (T2).\n\nThe degree-16 factors are `H^{16}(Mbar_{g_i,n_i};Q)`, occurring exactly in the summands `(a,b) = (16,0)` and `(0,16)`, and they have `n_i <= 7` by Step 4; Step 9 shows each such `(g_i,n_i)` lies outside RED-0001. Every other nonzero summand has both degrees even with `2 <= a,b <= 14`, inside the THM-0002 published tautological range, by Proposition K cases 1 and 2. These are (T3) and (T4). This proves the assigned theorem in full.\n\nSince there are finitely many one-edge separating stable graphs of `(6,6)` (finitely many genus splittings and leg partitions), the conclusion holds simultaneously for all of them, and `oplus_Gamma H^{16}([Mbar_Gamma/Aut(Gamma)];Q)` over the separating `Gamma` is a finite direct sum of `Q(-8)`.\n\n## 9. Clause (T3): disjointness from the RED-0001 residual list\n\nThe RED-0001 residual list is `(3,12),(5,8),(6,6),(7,4),(8,0),(8,1),(8,2)` (RED-0001 statement, cross-checked enumeration; FND-0035 records the same seven pairs). Their invariants are:\n\n| pair | `g` | `m = 2g+n` |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `(3,12)` | 3 | 18 |\n| `(5,8)` | 5 | 18 |\n| `(6,6)` | 6 | 18 |\n| `(7,4)` | 7 | 18 |\n| `(8,0)` | 8 | 16 |\n| `(8,1)` | 8 | 17 |\n| `(8,2)` | 8 | 18 |\n\nEvery pair on the list has `g >= 7` or `m = 18`. By Step 4 every vertex of a one-edge separating graph of `(6,6)` has `g_i <= 6` AND `m_i <= 17`; by Corollary M1 the same two inequalities hold for every stable pair occurring at a vertex of any stable graph of `(g_i,n_i)`, i.e. for every pair reachable in the degree-16 boundary induction of Lemma A. Hence no vertex, and no pair used anywhere in the induction that types it, belongs to the residual list. Both criteria are needed: `(8,0)` and `(8,1)` are excluded only by genus, and `(3,12),(5,8),(6,6)` only by `m`.\n\nTwo near misses show the statement is sharp rather than slack: the vertex `(6,5)` (paired with `(0,3)`) has `m = 17` and its non-separating degeneration is `(5,7)`, not the residual `(5,8)`; the vertex `(5,7)` (paired with `(1,1)`) has `m = 17` and `n = 7 = c(5)`. Both sit exactly on the CKgP boundary of Corollary M2.\n\nThis verifies the theorem's bookkeeping clause without using RED-0001's truth status: only its list of seven pairs is consumed, and the disjointness is proved by the numerical invariants `g` and `2g+n`.\n\n## 10. What is not claimed\n\n- The non-separating component `[Mbar_{5,8}/S_2]` is not treated. No statement is made about `H^*(Mbar_{5,8};Q)`, about its `S_2`-invariants or anti-invariants, or about the branch-swap determinant question of FND-0034 versus FC-REV0191-1. Note `(5,8)` is a RED-0001 residual pair and is excluded from every range used above; Lemma A is not applicable to it (`m(5,8) = 18 > 17`), and no attempt is made to extend it there.\n- The campaign boundary-image theorem is not concluded. Typing the separating components is one of three named inputs to C66BAR-IMAGE (the others being C66BAR-GRAPH and C58-COMPACT), and the image of the clutching pushforward is not computed here.\n- No conclusion is drawn about `H^{16}(Mbar_{6,6};Q)`, consistently with FND-0071 and FND-0072; nothing here bears on `W_{26}H^{26}(M_{6,6};Q)`, on the open residual, or on FND-0048.\n- *Consistency remark, not part of the theorem.* For a separating component `D_Gamma` the campaign's boundary map consumes `H_{16}(D_Gamma;Q) = H^{16}(D_Gamma;Q)^{dual}`, which by (T2) and (0.1) is a finite direct sum of `Q(8)`; no dimension-dependent twist is needed for this passage, and the alternative covariant description `H_{16}(D_Gamma;Q) ~= H^{24}(D_Gamma;Q)(20)` on the 20-dimensional component is consistent with it, since `H^{24} ~= (H^{16})^{dual}(-20)`. This is the degree/twist dictionary flagged as a candidate in REV-0191 (FC-REV0191-3); it is recorded here only to fix conventions and is not used in any proof step above, and it does not by itself say anything about the image of the boundary map.\n\n## 11. Blocked and failed routes\n\nThe four blocked routes of TASK.json are not used, and `new_inputs` is empty (nothing here reopens any of them).\n\n- `vcd-only-vanishing`: no step argues by vanishing above a virtual cohomological dimension. The open inputs in (7c) are made Tate by CKgP algebraicity (SRC-0002 Proposition 4.5) plus FND-0001 duality, never by `vcd` comparison; FND-0035 (vanishing above `vcd` proves no residual case) is respected, and indeed no residual case is claimed.\n- `undecorated-cgp-top-weight-graph-complex`: no tropical/CGP top-weight computation appears. Per FND-0032 the CGP corner is `gr^W_{2d}`, never the corner at issue; the present argument works with `W_kH^k` lowest-weight pieces and with compact cohomology of `Mbar`, and uses the boundary sequence THM-0003 rather than a graph complex. FND-0031's depth-one restriction is respected: only one-edge strata occur.\n- `point-count-without-degree-parity-separation`: no point counts over finite fields are used anywhere; degrees are separated by parity explicitly and by hand in Proposition K.\n- `forgetful-map-renaming-local-system-unknown`: no forgetful-map Leray argument, no renaming of an unknown local-system group; FND-0020 and FND-0037 are not invoked and no mixed `R^1` system appears.\n\nAdditional recorded hazards addressed. FND-0051 (stacky strata versus coarse versus invariants/coinvariants must be stated): Step 2 computes the group, `Aut(Gamma) = 1`, and Step 3 and (7a) use invariants over `Q` throughout, noting invariants equal coinvariants rationally; nothing is asserted integrally. FND-0034 (determinant twist): its separating-side content is used as stated, and its disputed non-separating parenthetical is neither used nor adjudicated, the conclusion being convention-free by Step 3. FND-0090 (Gysin/weight complex versus clutching-pullback kernel): only the boundary pushforward of THM-0003 is used; no simultaneous clutching-pullback kernel appears, and the two are not conflated. FND-0033: no gap marker is spent on ruling out exotic extensions at a single pure weight.\n\n## 12. Separation of proved steps, source-backed inputs, computational hypotheses, and gaps\n\n**Proved here (nothing cited):** Step 2 (`Aut(Gamma)=1`); Step 3 (vacuity of the determinant twist and the convention-free sub-object bound); Step 4 (`m_1+m_2=20`, `m_i <= 17`, `n_i <= 7`, `g_i <= 6`); Lemma M and Corollaries M1, M2 (Step 5); Proposition K (Step 6); Lemma A (Step 7), given its inputs; Steps 8 and 9.\n\n**Dependency artifacts consumed (verified, with both confirmations read):** ATT-0140 claims CLM-01, CLM-03, CLM-04, confirmed by REV-0189 and REV-0191. Their `(g,n)`-general form used in (7a) is re-derived there, the original proof being uniform in `(g,n)`.\n\n**Registry claims consumed as established source-backed inputs:** THM-0001, THM-0002, THM-0003, THM-0005, THM-0006, THM-0007, with the corpus locators quoted in Step 1.\n\n**Published theorems cited directly (not re-proved):** (S1) SRC-0002 Proposition 4.5 (`repo/corpus/text/SRC-0002.txt` L643-L648); (S2) SRC-0003 odd vanishing for odd `k <= 9` (`repo/corpus/text/SRC-0003.txt` L15-L16), used only for `k` in `{1,3,5,7}`. (S2) is the one load-bearing input carrying no registry claim id of its own; a reviewer should check it against SRC-0003 directly. It is used exactly once, in Proposition K case 1.\n\n**Adjudicated findings consumed:** FND-0001 (mechanically_verified) in (7c); FND-0033 in (P).\n\n**Computational hypotheses, experiments, finite verifications used to close a step:** none. The two finite enumerations that appear (the eight odd Kunneth bidegrees in Proposition K case 1, and the seven-pair table in Step 9) are exhaustive case splits of finite sets that are complete by the arithmetic constraint `a+b=16` and by the definition of the residual list, not empirical evidence.\n\n**Gaps:** none. Every step above is either proved here or is one of the enumerated cited inputs.\n",
